---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import TOOLKIT from "../data/toolkit.json";
const skills = await getCollection("skills");
const posts = (await getCollection("blog")).filter(post => !post.data.noindex);
---
<Base title="The LLMSX toolkit" description={TOOLKIT.description} route="/toolkit/">
  <p class="lede">Three ways to put the work to use: agent skills, local workbenches and source-linked context libraries.</p>
  <p>LLMSX is the umbrella. <code>llmsx</code> is the core research/context CLI; <code>skillsx</code> and <code>ghx</code> are separately installed companion workbenches. Choose the part you need.</p>
  <ul class="offering-grid">
    {TOOLKIT.pillars.map(pillar => <li><article><h2><a href={pillar.href}>{pillar.title}</a></h2><p>{pillar.body}</p><ul>{pillar.items.map(item => <li><a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a></li>)}</ul></article></li>)}
    <li><article><h2><a href="/family/">llms.txt family and tooling</a></h2><p>A site index, full export and three project extensions: small, facts and vocabulary. Inspect the <a href="/reference/attributes/">attribute rubric</a>, <a href="/reference/">technical reference</a> and <a href="/demo/">recorded retrieval comparison</a>. These files are served directly over HTTP. A lint result checks format and declared constraints; it does not certify every claim.</p></article></li>
    <li><article><h2><a href="/blog/">Empirical research and guides</a></h2><p>{posts.length} articles currently eligible for indexing, including controlled experiments, architecture accounts and hardware investigations. Read the setup, evidence and limits attached to each result. The <a href="/workflows/">worked workflows</a> connect a small task to a skill, reference and relevant app.</p></article></li>
    <li><article><h2>Account and hosted interfaces</h2><p><a href="/account/">Account</a>, <a href="/keys/">API keys</a>, <a href="/usage/">usage</a>, <a href="/queue/">research queue</a> and <a href="/proposals/">proposal</a> interfaces connect to the configured backend. They require their own service and account availability. The public files and local tools work independently; this page does not establish hosted-service availability.</p></article></li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What a skill run means</h2><p>The library currently presents {skills.length} reusable skill prompts. An optimizer specifies a bounded review-and-fix procedure with checks, iteration limits and unresolved-finding behavior. A capable agent must execute those instructions; installing a SKILL.md or making a single model call does not itself run a multi-pass loop.</p>
  <p>Skill pages include a copyable install command, full prompt, illustrative before/after view, prompt-size estimate and calculator for your own billable usage. No fixed run cost or zero-defect outcome is promised.</p>
  <h2>Start with one artifact</h2><p><a href="/skills/">Copy a skill</a>, <a href="/apps/">install a workbench</a> or <a href="/context/">download context</a>. Use the <a href="/tree/">Concept tree</a> to find a subject and the <a href="/editorial/">editorial standards</a> to interpret its evidence.</p>
</Base>
